  "query_preview": "..\n\nWinds tonight into Friday: Wind advisories have been issued for\nnorth- central WA for a strong cold front moving in overnight \ninto Friday morning delivering strong north to northwest winds\nof 10-30 mph and gusts 40-50 mph. HREF probabilities for wind \ngusts greater than 40 mph are near 100% for some areas with \n25th percentile wind gusts between 40-50 mph for isolated \nareas. Winds will increase after midnight with peak gusts \n3AM-11AM. Main concerns during these winds will be isolated tree\ndamage and cross winds along Highway 2 between Creston and \nDouglas as the strongest winds for the Waterville Plateau and\nColumbia basin will be focused over northwestern Douglas County,\nNorthern Grant County, and western Lincoln County. Winds will \nremain gusty through the afternoon as mentioned in the \ndiscussion below with concerns for patchy blowing dust and \nelevated fire weather conditions. \n\nThursday through Friday: A low off the California coast will \neject northeast into California, Nevada and southern Idaho. While \nthe system is passing well to our south, some of this moisture \ngets picked up by an incoming upper trough from the west, with \nprecipitable water values increasing to 175-200 percent of normal \nacross the eastern third of Washington into the Idaho Panhandle. \nThis will translate to a band of rain moving into the region, with\nmodels trending wetter with rain amounts of 0.10-0.30 inches from\nthe Blue Mountains and Palouse northward across the Spokane area,\nNortheast Mountains, and ID Panhandle. Also aiding in \nprecipitation will be mid level instability with 700-500mb MU CAPE\nvalues of 50-100 J/KG. Timing of rain for Eastern WA is mainly \nduring the afternoon and evening hours, becoming more confined to \nthe Idaho Panhandle overnight into early Friday. As the trough\naxis moves over the region tonight, cooler and drier air moves\ninto Central WA as a cold front passes. The dry air really becomes\nnoteworthy Friday afternoon as precipitable water values plummet \nto 25-50 percent of normal. This combined with breezy north- \nnorthwest winds (locally windy down the Okanogan Valley and \nWaterville Plateau) will promote elevated fire weather conditions\nand the potential for patchy blowing dust. Winds ease Friday \nNight, and with the very dry air over the region radiational \ncooling will be strong, with freezing temperatures for most \n(except locally around Wenatchee), with the colder pockets such as\nRepublic and Deer Park falling into the low 20s.\n\nSaturday through Wednesday: Ensembles to continue to struggle \nwith a low that strengthens off the southern BC coast on Saturday \nand then what happens after that. 45 percent of the clusters\nposition the low by Monday near the WA coast for cooler and\nshowery weather, especially near the Cascades. 20 percent take the\nlow directly over the region for much cooler and widespread\nshowery weather, as well as mountain snow. The remaining 35\npercent have the low much further west and maintain high pressure\nover the region. Given the uncertainty not too many changes to \nthis part of the forecast which currently provides a middle ground\nof the model solutions with near seasonal temperatures with highs\nmainly in the 50s and a 20-30 percent chance of showers. But with\na 10-15 degree range between the NBM 10th and 90th percentiles \nthere is low confidence in the forecast details. Washington Pass\nSunday and Sunday Night has a 20 percent chance of 1 inch or more\nof snow. JW",
